{
  "edition": "September 2026",
  "updated": "2026-11-01",
  "title": "Where India's money is going",
  "intro": "Every month the fund industry publishes what the country actually did with its money: what it bought, what it sold, which fund houses grew. Here it is, without the jargon.",
  "take": {
    "asof": "2026-11-01",
    "heading": "What to make of it",
    "body": "The market fell and the money stayed. That is the whole month in a line, and it is a change worth noticing: for most of the last two decades Indian investors sold into a fall. The big debt swings are corporate treasuries moving cash around a quarter end, not households changing their minds. Watch the equity and SIP rows for what people like you are doing, and leave the debt row to the finance departments it belongs to."
  },
  "how_to_read": "Debt swings are quarter-end money moving in and out of liquid and money market funds. That is company treasuries parking cash, not families investing. The equity and SIP columns are the household story.",
  "equity_note": "Small and mid caps keep leading, which is the opposite of what a falling market usually produces. ELSS has been in net outflow for months, and that is the tax regime rather than the funds: if you no longer claim 80C, the lock-in buys you nothing.",
  "passive_note": "Gold and silver are doing the heavy lifting in passive right now. Worth knowing before you read the headline number as proof that India has discovered index funds.",
